From 70c24c9098daf536af2faa769312f8be76c987a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bill Hlavacek <bill.hlavacek@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 15:09:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] bngsim: count a whole-complex deletion and a MatchOnce
 reactant once per complex

A rule that deletes a whole species (no DeleteMolecules) removes the complex
its match sits in, and BioNetGen gives it one reaction per complex at the
rule's rate, however many matches the complex holds. The reactant list holds
one mapping set per match, so NFsim fired it once per match: a homodimer at
2k, a 4-ring at 4k, M() -> 0 on M.N.M at 2k. MatchOnce asks for the same
per-complex counting, and its count through countDistinctComplexes() never
took effect: a list is only noted as able to share a complex for a pure
context reactant. NFinput dropped MatchOnce on a DOR rule outright.

fire() now fires such a pick with probability 1/m, m the mapping sets of the
reactant's list rooted in the picked complex, counted over the complex's
member list where complexes are tracked and by bond traversal where they are
not: a complex goes at the rule's rate once, and complexes are picked evenly
whatever their matches. Not under a total rate, nor for a reactant already
counted per complex. It draws only where a complex holds two or more matches,
so other runs keep their random streams. MatchOnce is applied to DOR rules
too, and leaves the per-complex count paths, which it never reached. See
bngsim issue #1525.
